The lighthouse keeper had a peculiar habit of writing down every unusual sound he heard during the night watch. Most entries were mundane: gulls crying in the distance, wind rattling the shutters, waves striking the rocks below. But one evening, just after midnight, he heard three slow knocks from somewhere beneath the lighthouse itself. There was no basement, no tunnel, and no machinery beneath the stone floor. He wrote the time in his logbook anyway, underlining it twice.

Far beyond the harbor, the town was quietly settling into sleep. Porch lights clicked off one by one, cars disappeared from the streets, and the last train rolled north through the valley. Yet above all of it, the lighthouse continued its patient rotation, sweeping the darkness with a narrow beam of light. Each time the beam crossed the water, something distant flashed back—not brightly enough to be called a signal, but regularly enough to make the keeper wonder whether the sea was simply reflecting the light, or answering it.

